=== A. Regulation T payment period is settlement cycle plus two business days ===
standard settlement cycle T+1; payment period = 3 business days
cash-account purchase on 2026-07-06 has payment period ending 2026-07-09
=== B. Freeriding and the 90-calendar-day freeze ===
sold on 2026-07-07 before paying in full: 90-calendar-day freeze ends 2026-10-05
during the freeze, new purchases must be paid for on the trade date (the delay-beyond-trade-date privilege is withdrawn)
=== C. Same sale, but paid with other funds inside the payment period; proceeds not withdrawn ===
full payment 2026-07-08 (on or before 2026-07-09); proceeds withdrawn? False -> freeze? False
selling before T+1 settlement is not automatically freeriding if you pay with other funds on time and do not take the sale proceeds first
=== D. TXSE official sessions (Eastern Time) ===
  08:00 ET -> pre-market
  09:29 ET -> pre-market
  09:30 ET -> regular
  15:59 ET -> regular
  16:00 ET -> post-market
  16:59 ET -> post-market
  17:00 ET -> closed
  07:59 ET -> closed
SIP participant / market-center identifier for TXSE is F; pre-launch tape data will not contain F prints
=== E. Rule 15c3-3 reserve toy (credits minus debits, not the full Exhibit A formula) ===
customer credits 100000 - debits 40000 -> reserve at least 60000
the carrying broker must also keep possession or control of fully paid and excess-margin securities; this is separate from SIPC
assertions passed: 14/14
=== F. Rule 15c6-1 default and FINRA 11320 delivery-date types ===
regular way: trade 2026-07-06 delivers on, but not before, 2026-07-07
cash: delivery on the day of the transaction 2026-07-06
delayed-delivery: date agreed at the time of the transaction 2026-07-10
11320(e): contract due 2026-07-11 (not a business day) matures 2026-07-13
15c6-1(c) firm-commitment offering priced after 4:30 p.m. ET: not forced into (a)'s first-business-day default; up to 2026-07-08 unless otherwise agreed
T+1 is the 15c6-1(a) default unless the parties expressly agree otherwise at the time of the transaction
do not write that every trade must settle T+1, that a cash contract settles T+1, or that every IPO or follow-on settles T+2
current FINRA 11320 has no separate next-day category (amended by SR-FINRA-2023-017 eff. May 28, 2024)
11320 also lists seller's option and buyer's option; 11320(g)-(h) delivery time-and-place rules exist; this toy is not that engine
a not-regular-way contract can be a listed Rule 611(b)(2) trade-through exception; that does not make every such print a data error
assertions passed: 19/19
disclaimer: learning only; not a UPC or close-out engine; no product or venue recommendation
=== G. FINRA 2266: written SIPC information at account opening and at least annually ===
2266: All members, except those members: (a) that pursuant to Section 3(a)(2)(A)(i) through (iii)
  of the Securities Investor Protection Act of 1970 (SIPA) are excluded from membership in the
  Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC) and that are not SIPC members; or (b) whose
  business consists exclusively of the sale of investments that are ineligible for SIPC protection,
  shall advise all new customers, in writing, at the opening of an account, that they may obtain
  information about SIPC, including the SIPC brochure, by contacting SIPC, and also shall provide
  the Web site address and telephone number of SIPC.
  In addition, such members shall provide all customers with the same information, in writing,
  at least once each year.
  In cases where both an introducing firm and clearing firm service an account,
  the firms may assign these requirements to one of the firms
official typography is "Web site" as two words
official (a)-(b) exceptions exist; this toy is not a SIPA-exclusion engine
  this is a rewrite of already-taught SIPC coverage limits? False
  this toy invents a coverage amount, form, or clock this rule does not state? False
  written information is required at account opening and at least once each year? True
no broker or SIPC-membership recommendation
assertions passed: 22/22
